// Accepted: `desc` marks a key value as ordered by its dual (ADR 0031,
// Decision 7).  Because the marker sits on *values* rather than on the
// operator, direction is per-component, which is what a global reverse flag
// could not express.
//
// In `formal/` the marker is Mathlib's `OrderDual`, so the arrangement absorbs
// it at no proof cost and Tier 1 survives it: the dual of a total order is
// total, and the dual of an injective key is injective.
//
// This file is also where `assume { arranged }` earns its keep.  Ranking by
// temperature is a genuinely tie-prone order: two readings of one machine may
// well agree to the stored precision, and then no arrangement is canonical, so
// the ranks of the tied rows depend on how the rows happened to be stored.  The
// checker cannot rule that out (injectivity is a property of the data), and no
// grading covers `temperature`, so the claim is admitted here locally and
// visibly, exactly as `assume { complete }` admits completeness (ADR 0017,
// whose block form was written to generalize this way; ADR 0029 Decision 11's
// tier 3).
//
// Contrast `qualified_series_windows.mensura`, where the order key is a
// projected key component and needs no claim at all
// (`Mensura.keyInjOn_demote_tag`).  Preferring that shape is the point: the
// claim is for orders that really are ambiguous, not a line to paste.
import series

unit Machine {
  id: string
}

store readings {
  unit { Machine }
  attr* { temperature: real }
}

view ranked {
  readings
    |> assume { arranged }
    // `rank` and the `>>`-scan are fold-admitting, so both demand
    // completeness over the current key (ADR 0037 decision 5).  A
    // presence-relative rank over a bag store remains expressible,
    // deliberately, under exactly this visible claim.
    |> assume { complete }
    |> map_bags |k, b| (
      // A descending `rank` needs no binding of its own: it is the ones-scan
      // over a `desc` key at the call site (Decision 8).
      .hottest_first = series.rank (|r| desc r.temperature) b,
      // The marker composes with a plain scan too.
      .peak_desc = scan `>>` (|r| r.temperature) (|r| desc r.temperature) b
    )
}
